<p dir="auto">Suggested fix: standardize Codex offsets (either store <code class="notranslate">CheckpointTranscriptStart</code> as response_item index everywhere, or make the Codex slicing function accept raw line offsets to match existing metadata semantics).</p>"}, {"url": "https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/875#discussion_r3053483581", "body": "`SliceFromResponseItem` defines the offset `n` in units of “response_item entries”, but other Codex transcript code uses offsets as raw JSONL line numbers. This mismatch makes it easy to pass the wrong offset (e.g. `CheckpointTranscriptStart`) and get incorrect scoping.

Also, this implementation splits/trim/re-joins JSONL, which changes the original bytes (drops empty lines/whitespace and trailing newline) and allocates a new buffer. Consider implementing the slice by computing a byte offset into the original `data` while scanning lines, so the returned transcript preserves original content exactly.
```suggestion
// SliceFromResponseItem returns the transcript content after skipping n JSONL
// lines. The returned suffix preserves the original bytes exactly.
func SliceFromResponseItem(data []byte, n int) ([]byte, error) {
	if len(data) == 0 || n <= 0 {
		return data, nil
	}

	linesSkipped := 0
	lineStart := 0

	for i, b := range data {
		if b != '\n' {
			continue
		}

		linesSkipped++
		lineStart = i + 1
		if linesSkipped >= n {
			if lineStart >= len(data) {
				return nil, nil
			}
			return data[lineStart:], nil
		}
	}

	if lineStart < len(data) {
		linesSkipped++
		if linesSkipped >= n {
			return nil, nil
		}
	}
