comments.body:
`SliceFromResponseItem` defines the offset `n` in units of “response_item entries”, but other Codex transcript code uses offsets as raw JSONL line numbers. This mismatch makes it easy to pass the wrong offset (e.g. `CheckpointTranscriptStart`) and get incorrect scoping.

Also, this implementation splits/trim/re-joins JSONL, which changes the original bytes (drops empty lines/whitespace and trailing newline) and allocates a new buffer. Consider implementing the slice by computing a byte offset into the original `data` while scanning lines, so the returned transcript preserves original content exactly.
```suggestion
// SliceFromResponseItem returns the transcript content after skipping n JSONL
// lines. The returned suffix preserves the original bytes exactly.
func SliceFromResponseItem(data []byte, n int) ([]byte, error) {
	if len(data) == 0 || n <= 0 {
		return data, nil
	}

	linesSkipped := 0
	lineStart := 0

	for i, b := range data {
		if b != '\n' {
			continue
		}

		linesSkipped++
		lineStart = i + 1
		if linesSkipped >= n {
			if lineStart >= len(data) {
				return nil, nil
			}
			return data[lineStart:], nil
		}
	}

	if lineStart < len(data) {
		linesSkipped++
		if linesSkipped >= n {
			return nil, nil
		}
	}
```

This test transcript contains only `session_meta` + `response_item` lines, so a raw “line offset” and a “response_item offset” behave similarly and won’t catch scoping bugs when real Codex rollouts include `event_msg` / `turn_context` lines. Consider adding at least one non-response_item line (e.g. an `event_msg` token_count) between response_items so the test validates the intended offset semantics.
```suggestion
{"timestamp":"t5","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"response to first"}]}}
{"timestamp":"t5.5","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"token_count","input_tokens":12,"output_tokens":7}}
```

The new Codex condensation test only covers transcripts that start with `session_meta`. A key scenario for explain --generate is checkpoint-scoped Codex transcripts that start mid-file (often `response_item` or `event_msg`), so it would be good to add a subtest where the input omits the `session_meta` header (and/or starts with an `event_msg token_count`) to verify format detection + compaction still works.
