Suggested fix: standardize Codex offsets (either store `CheckpointTranscriptStart` as response_item index everywhere, or make the Codex slicing function accept raw line offsets to match existing metadata semantics).","user":{"login":"Copilot","name":"Copilot","email":null,"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/946600?v=4","id":175728472},"id":3053483527,"pull_request_review_id":4077478924,"in_reply_to_id":null,"created_at":"2026-04-08T18:47:53Z","review":null,"side":"RIGHT","line":null,"start_line":null,"path":"cmd/entire/cli/explain.go","body_html":"<p dir="auto"><code class="notranslate">scopeTranscriptForCheckpoint</code> passes <code class="notranslate">CheckpointTranscriptStart</code> into <code class="notranslate">codex.SliceFromResponseItem</code>, but <code class="notranslate">CheckpointTranscriptStart</code> is documented/used elsewhere as a raw transcript line offset for JSONL agents. <code class="notranslate">SliceFromResponseItem</code> instead interprets the offset as “number of response_item entries”, which will mis-scope Codex transcripts that include non-response_item lines (e.g. event_msg token_count) and can cause explain --generate to summarize the wrong portion.</p>
<p dir="auto">Suggested fix: standardize Codex offsets (either store <code class="notranslate">CheckpointTranscriptStart</code> as response_item index everywhere, or make the Codex slicing function accept raw line offsets to match existing metadata semantics).</p>"},{"url":"https://github.com/entireio/cli/pull/875#discussion_r3053483581","body":"`SliceFromResponseItem` defines the offset `n` in units of “response_item entries”, but other Codex transcript code uses offsets as raw JSONL line numbers. This mismatch makes it easy to pass the wrong offset (e.g. `CheckpointTranscriptStart`) and get incorrect scoping.

Also, this implementation splits/trim/re-joins JSONL, which changes the original bytes (drops empty lines/whitespace and trailing newline) and allocates a new buffer. Consider implementing the slice by computing a byte offset into the original `data` while scanning lines, so the returned transcript preserves original content exactly.
```suggestion
// SliceFromResponseItem returns the transcript content after skipping n JSONL
// lines. The returned suffix preserves the original bytes exactly.
func SliceFromResponseItem(data []byte, n int) ([]byte, error) {
	if len(data) == 0 || n <= 0 {
		return data, nil
	}

	linesSkipped := 0
	lineStart := 0

	for i, b := range data {
		if b != '\n' {
			continue
		}

		linesSkipped++
		lineStart = i + 1
		if linesSkipped >= n {
			if lineStart >= len(data) {
				return nil, nil
			}
			return data[lineStart:], nil
		}
	}

	if lineStart < len(data) {
		linesSkipped++
		if linesSkipped >= n {
			return nil, nil
		}
	}
